WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:03.400
If deep time is incomprehensible,

00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:11.960
how does the human brain understand the deep time over which it is now believed to have evolved?

00:00:11.960 --> 00:00:16.200
This is a tricky question, you've got to read 
this a couple times before you understand

00:00:16.200 --> 00:00:19.620
what it means. Basically Darwin asks us

00:00:19.620 --> 00:00:22.900
to imagine that human beings along with

00:00:22.900 --> 00:00:26.900
other animals and plants emerged over deep time.

00:00:26.900 --> 00:00:29.800
That over millions and millions of years

00:00:29.800 --> 00:00:33.900
human beings changed from
essentially apes and monkeys

00:00:33.900 --> 00:00:36.020
into the people that we have today.

00:00:36.020 --> 00:00:39.040
The human brain is therefore

00:00:39.040 --> 00:00:41.680
not just a product of evolution.

00:00:41.680 --> 00:00:44.420
But it is a product of deep time.

00:00:44.420 --> 00:00:49.560
How does the brain understand the deep time

00:00:49.560 --> 00:00:52.500
over which it is believed to have evolved.

00:00:52.500 --> 00:00:56.200
So this also requires conceptualization.

00:00:56.200 --> 00:01:00.400
People had been thinking about the brain
for several hundred years.

00:01:00.400 --> 00:01:05.060
This is an example of how you can look at
different kinds of brains.

00:01:05.060 --> 00:01:07.140
Over the course of the 18th century.

00:01:07.140 --> 00:01:09.920
So comparing brains thinking about brains.

00:01:09.920 --> 00:01:11.880
This idea is not new.

00:01:11.880 --> 00:01:15.000
But what you have in the 19th century

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:20.520
is the first effort to understand the human brain
in geological terms

00:01:20.520 --> 00:01:23.040
as the product of deep time.

00:01:23.040 --> 00:01:26.780
So they imagine that there's a lower part of the brain

00:01:26.780 --> 00:01:28.940
that is very very primitive.

00:01:28.940 --> 00:01:33.000
And it can only understand space in terms of time.

00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:37.380
That is it takes me three days to walk to Menlo Park.

00:01:37.380 --> 00:01:39.960
That is how far Menlo Park is from here.

00:01:39.960 --> 00:01:42.300
It takes me three days to walk there.

00:01:42.300 --> 00:01:45.740
That's the primitive part of the brain,
so they think in the 19th century.

00:01:45.740 --> 00:01:52.240
Over time the higher parts of the brain
like geological strata accreting

00:01:52.240 --> 00:01:55.840
begin to get deposited in the human mind.

00:01:55.840 --> 00:02:00.200
Leading to our modern advanced brains.

00:02:00.200 --> 00:02:02.940
How do we know they're modern and advanced?

00:02:02.940 --> 00:02:07.140
Is that we can imagine time in terms of space.

00:02:07.140 --> 00:02:08.900
We have things like clocks

00:02:08.900 --> 00:02:12.360
that help us to see time in spatial terms.

00:02:12.360 --> 00:02:15.500
And this is a sign of how advanced we are.

00:02:15.500 --> 00:02:18.980
So the human brain begins to be imagined

00:02:18.980 --> 00:02:22.580
like a geological stratum in the 19th century

00:02:22.580 --> 00:02:27.080
that it's strata like rocks are layered through time.

00:02:27.540 --> 00:02:35.500
Now let me show you how that idea has crept 
into our way of thinking and talking today

00:02:35.500 --> 00:02:37.940
in ways that we didn't even know

00:02:37.940 --> 00:02:45.260
Many of us, all of you in this room, have certainly
used the term the stream of consciousness.

00:02:45.260 --> 00:02:48.440
And you have probably used it without understanding

00:02:48.440 --> 00:02:53.540
the origins of this term.
Well it emerges from the deep time revolution

00:02:53.540 --> 00:02:57.280
in the writing of the psychologist William James

00:02:57.280 --> 00:03:01.600
who studied with geologist Louis Agassiz at Harvard.

00:03:01.600 --> 00:03:06.200
He was very influenced by this
new deep time way of thinking.

00:03:06.200 --> 00:03:09.360
Not just about the earth as layered strata,

00:03:09.360 --> 00:03:12.580
but about the human brain as layered strata.

00:03:12.580 --> 00:03:23.799
He tried to explain how you could understand the flow of human thought as a kind of geological process.

00:03:23.799 --> 00:03:26.880
So he asks you to imagine

00:03:26.880 --> 00:03:29.000
saying in this sentence:

00:03:29.000 --> 00:03:32.480
"the pack of cards is on the table."

00:03:32.480 --> 00:03:38.000
This is gonna bring back traumatic memories of diagramming sentences in seventh grade.

00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:41.000
So I'm sorry because it brought those back for me.

00:03:41.000 --> 00:03:45.280
Sorry Mrs. Collie, I didn't like diagramming sentences.

00:03:45.280 --> 00:03:47.580
But William James is asking us to imagine,

00:03:47.580 --> 00:03:50.600
and I actually I brought a prop so here look at this.

00:03:50.600 --> 00:03:53.980
The pack of cards is on the table.

00:03:53.980 --> 00:03:59.480
And he's asking you to diagram what the brain is doing

00:03:59.480 --> 00:04:02.360
as it is thinking that thought.

00:04:02.360 --> 00:04:05.580
And he calls this the stream of consciousness.

00:04:05.580 --> 00:04:07.640
Here's what he's imagining.

00:04:07.640 --> 00:04:10.320
Remember the X-Y axis I showed you earlier in the talk?

00:04:10.320 --> 00:04:12.560
This is exactly what he's doing.

00:04:12.560 --> 00:04:16.580
He's imagining you saying this sentence:

00:04:16.580 --> 00:04:20.900
"The pack of cards is on the table."

00:04:20.900 --> 00:04:24.780
And the 1 2 3 and 4 is not grammatical right?

00:04:24.780 --> 00:04:29.020
It's just like time. Time is happening: 1-2-3-4.

00:04:29.020 --> 00:04:33.520
And then he has this y-axis this spatial dimension.

00:04:33.520 --> 00:04:38.160
And what the space is,
is not a kind of geological stratum.

00:04:38.160 --> 00:04:41.700
But it is what he calls the fullness of thought.

00:04:41.700 --> 00:04:44.400
So as you're saying the sentence,

00:04:44.400 --> 00:04:46.700
and as you're thinking it

00:04:46.700 --> 00:04:50.680
your mind is getting fuller and fuller and fuller

00:04:50.680 --> 00:04:55.100
of the thought of the pack of cards being on the table.

00:04:55.100 --> 00:04:57.780
So that at the end of the sentence

00:04:57.780 --> 00:05:01.440
you're way up high with your thinking

00:05:01.440 --> 00:05:04.400
about the pack of cards being on the table.

00:05:04.400 --> 00:05:08.200
Now what's interesting about this is that he says

00:05:08.200 --> 00:05:12.880
if you put a knife through the center of the thought—

00:05:12.880 --> 00:05:16.040
imagine doing that, put a knife
through the center of the thought.

00:05:16.040 --> 00:05:22.620
You would find it suffused with the whole of all
of the rest of the thought.

00:05:22.620 --> 00:05:26.900
Just like a knife cutting through the strata of the rocks

00:05:26.900 --> 00:05:28.300
that I have been showing you

00:05:28.300 --> 00:05:33.820
would show you cutting through all
of the strata of the earth.

00:05:33.820 --> 00:05:38.260
So when you use the term the stream of consciousness

00:05:38.260 --> 00:05:41.460
he means a stream in the literal

00:05:41.460 --> 00:05:43.420
geological sense of the term.

00:05:43.420 --> 00:05:47.980
That our brains have thoughts
running through them like streams

00:05:47.980 --> 00:06:04.989
that erode through our minds and that
we can diagram them like a geological stratum.

